From 5905737ca10d107eeef93227e2b0de7693820baa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Medium1992 <122653016+Medium1992@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 02:21:41 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 11/12] XHTTP: Make the HTTP/2 flow governor opt-in and
 configurable

The governor is now off unless XRAY_XHTTP_FLOW=on, and each inbound or
outbound can decide for itself in "extra":

    "h2Flow": {
        "enabled": true,
        "maxStreamReceiveWindow": 6291456,
        "maxConnectionReceiveWindow": 6291456
    }

"enabled" overrides the environment. The windows are what this side
grants for data it receives, for HTTP/2 over TCP only.

Go's server grants 1 MiB per connection, so every upload on one XHTTP
connection is capped at 1 MiB per round trip. A governed server now
grants 6 MiB unless configured, the most Linux autotuning lets one TCP
socket buffer; the governor still holds each stream to what is read.
Clients keep Go's 4 MiB per stream unless configured, so bridges can
raise it while small routers do not.

Clients without this change ignore "h2Flow".
